  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 438 AM MST Mon Nov 9 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nZonal flow aloft persists over the CWA today, allowing for \nseasonally average temperatures and mild winds. An upper level \nshortwave trough moves through the area tonight through tomorrow \nafternoon, bringing a surge of moisture with it. This will allow \nfor low, but non-zero, chances of precipitation tonight scattered \nacross the region, particularly over higher elevations and across \nthe Golden Triangle. However, snow levels still remain above 6000 \nft. \n\nIncreased moisture aloft is not the only thing the passage of \nthis shortwave trough is bringing. It is also bringing another \nround of windy conditions on Tuesday, due to causing a tightening \npressure gradient, both at the surface and aloft. These winds are \nnot expected to be as strong as those this past weekend, with \ncurrent forecasted gusts only being around 60-65 mph across the \nRocky Mountain Front and 40-45 mph throughout the rest of North-\nCentral MT. \n\nBy Tuesday night a strong upper level ridge begins to build over \nthe Idaho Panhandle, and will slowly move east until its axis is \ncentered over the Montana-Dakotas border by Friday afternoon. This\nwill allow for temperatures to rise to seasonally warm values and\nfor winds to mellow out, although relatively breezy stretches are\nstill possible across the plains. However, it is hard to go more \nthan a few days without windy conditions over Central/North- \nCentral MT, and this case holds true once again for this weekend.\nThe ridge begins to break down Saturday morning, and combined \nwith another passage of a shortwave trough Saturday evening, will \nbring the potential for windy conditions to return over the \nweekend. Uncertainty still exists in how this ridge will \nbreakdown, so not much can be said at this time in regards to how \nstrong these winds may be. -Dzomba\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nAs mentioned prior, cross barrier flow increases once again as \nthe next wave progresses through the region. Orientation of the \nstronger flow aloft (southwesterly) is more favorable late Monday \nevening, though ridgetop stability will be weaker compared to \nTuesday. This raises doubts that stronger winds will materialize \nat the surface near the Rocky Mountain Front Monday evening/night.\nAs better ridgetop stability develops into Tuesday the stronger \nwinds aloft trend more northwesterly. This orientation is less \nconducive to mixing stronger winds to the surface, but it will \nbear monitoring. -AM\n\nThere is a 50-70% chance of gusts greater than 65 MPH along the \nimmediate eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front on Tuesday, \nwith those chances decreasing to only 20-40% for gusts greater \nthan 75 MPH. Winds throughout the rest of Central/North-Central MT\nwill be much less, with considerable chances dropping off rapidly\nbeyond gusts of 40 MPH. The exception is the area of Great Falls \nsouth to the Little Belts, which still has a 40-60% chance of \ngusts greater than 50 MPH. -Dzomba",
